                "role": "assistant",
                "content": "**Notation**\n\n* \\(r\\) \u2013 distance from the centre  \n* \\(\\ell=m r^{2}\\dot\\phi\\;(>0)\\) \u2013 conserved angular momentum  \n* \\(u\\equiv 1/r\\) \u2013 reciprocal radius  \n\nThe given central potential  \n\n\\[\nV(r)=-\\frac{k}{r}+\\frac{\\lambda}{2r^{2}},\\qquad k>0,\\;\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Effective potential and circular\u2011orbit condition  \n\nThe kinetic energy associated with the motion in the plane is  \n\n\\[\nT=\\frac12 m\\bigl(\\dot r^{\\,2}+r^{2}\\dot\\phi^{\\,2}\\bigr)\n      =\\frac12 m\\dot r^{\\,2}+ \\frac{m\\ell^{2}}{2r^{4}} .\n\\]\n\nHence the **effective potential**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{%\nV_{\\rm eff}(r)=V(r)+\\frac{m\\ell^{2}}{2r^{4}}\n               =-\\frac{k}{r}\n                +\\frac{\\lambda}{2r^{2}}\n                +\\frac{m\\ell^{2}}{2r^{4}} } .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Circular orbit  \n\nA circular orbit of radius \\(r_{0}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}V_{\\rm eff}}{{\\rm d}r}\\Big|_{r=r_{0}}=0 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}V_{\\rm eff}}{{\\rm d}r}\n    =\\frac{k}{r^{2}}-\\frac{\\lambda+m\\ell^{2}/r^{2}}{r^{3}}=0\n    \\Longrightarrow \n    k\\,r_{0}= \\lambda+\\frac{m\\ell^{2}}{r_{0}^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(r_{0}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{%\nr_{0}= \\frac{\\displaystyle \\lambda+\\dfrac{m\\ell^{2}}{k}}{k}\n     =\\frac{\\lambda}{k}+ \\frac{m\\ell^{2}}{k^{2}} } .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Stability  \n\nStability requires the second derivative to be positive:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}^{2}V_{\\rm eff}}{{\\rm d}r^{2}}\\Big|_{r_{0}}\n   =-\\frac{2k}{r_{0}^{3}}+\\frac{3(\\lambda+m\\ell^{2}/r_{0}^{2})}{r_{0}^{4}}\n   =\\frac{k\\,(3-2r_{0})}{r_{0}^{4}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}^{2}V_{\\rm eff}}{{\\rm d}r^{2}}\\Big|_{r_{0}}>0\n\\iff 3-2r_{0}>0\n\\iff r_{0}<\\frac32 .\n\\]\n\nUsing the expression for \\(r_{0}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{%\n\\lambda+\\frac{m\\ell^{2}}{k}<\\frac{3k}{2}}\n\\qquad\\text{(condition for a stable circular orbit)} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   Binet equation for the orbit  \n\nWith \\(u=1/r\\) the Binet formula reads  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}^{2}u}{{\\rm d}\\phi^{2}}+u=\n-\\frac{m}{\\ell^{2}u^{2}}\\,F\\!\\left(\\frac1u\\right),\n\\qquad \nF(r)=-\\frac{{\\rm d}V}{{\\rm d}r}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the potential  \n\n\\[\nF(r)=-\\frac{{\\rm d}}{{\\rm d}r}\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{k}{r}+\\frac{\\lambda}{2r^{2}}\\Bigr)\n     =-\\frac{k}{r^{2}}+\\frac{\\lambda}{r^{3}} .\n\\]\n\nReplacing \\(r=1/u\\),\n\n\\[\nF\\!\\left(\\frac1u\\right)= -k u^{2}+ \\lambda u^{3}=u^{2}\\bigl(-k+\\lambda u\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}^{2}u}{{\\rm d}\\phi^{2}}+u=\n\\frac{m}{\\ell^{2}}\\bigl(k-\\lambda u\\bigr)\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\boxed{%\n\\frac{{\\rm d}^{2}u}{{\\rm d}\\phi^{2}}+\n\\Bigl[1+\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\ell^{2}}\\Bigr]u=\n\\frac{mk}{\\ell^{2}} } .\n\\]\n\nIntroduce  \n\n\\[\n\\nu^{2}\\equiv 1+\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\ell^{2}},\n\\qquad (\\nu>0\\; \\text{for a real orbit}),\n\\]\n\nso the equation becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}^{2}u}{{\\rm d}\\phi^{2}}+\\nu^{2}u=\\frac{mk}{\\ell^{2}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   Bounded (elliptic\u2011type) solution  \n\nThe general solution of the linear ODE is  \n\n\\[\nu(\\phi)=C_{1}\\cos(\\nu\\phi)+C_{2}\\sin(\\nu\\phi)+\\frac{mk}{\\ell^{2}\\nu^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nWriting it in the usual \u201cKepler\u2011like\u2019\u2019 form  \n\n\\[\nu(\\phi)=u_{0}\\Bigl[1+e\\cos\\bigl(\\nu(\\phi-\\phi_{0})\\bigr)\\Bigr],\n\\]\n\nwe identify  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{%\nu_{0}= \\frac{mk}{\\ell^{2}\\,\\nu^{2}},\\qquad \n\\nu=\\sqrt{1+\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\ell^{2}}}} .\n\\]\n\n(The constants \\(e\\) and \\(\\phi_{0}\\) are fixed by the initial conditions.)\n\n---\n\n**Azimuthal advance between two successive periapsides**\n\nPeriapse occurs when the cosine term equals\u202f\\(1\\); the next periapse is reached when the argument has increased by \\(2\\pi\\):\n\n\\[\n\\nu\\,\\Delta\\phi = 2\\pi\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\boxed{\\Delta\\phi = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\nu}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   Periapsis precession per radial period  \n\nOne radial period corresponds to one complete oscillation of \\(r\\), i.e. an increase of the true anomaly by \\(2\\pi\\).  \nBecause the angular coordinate actually changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta\\phi_{\\text{rad}} = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\nu},\n\\]\n\nthe **precession per radial period** (the extra angle beyond \\(2\\pi\\)) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\delta\\phi = 2\\pi-\\frac{2\\pi}{\\nu}=2\\pi\\Bigl(1-\\frac1\\nu\\Bigr)} .\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(\\nu=\\sqrt{1+\\varepsilon}\\) with \\(\\displaystyle\\varepsilon\\equiv\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\ell^{2}}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\delta\\phi = 2\\pi\\Bigl(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+\\varepsilon}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(|\\varepsilon|\\ll1\\) we expand  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+\\varepsilon}}=1-\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}+O(\\varepsilon^{2}),\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\delta\\phi \\simeq 2\\pi\\left(\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\right)\n        =\\pi\\,\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\ell^{2}} }\n        \\qquad\\bigl(|\\varepsilon|\\ll1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n* **Sign of the precession**  \n\n  * If \\(\\lambda>0\\) (\\(\\varepsilon>0\\)), \\(\\delta\\phi>0\\): the periapsis **advances** (prograde precession).  \n  * If \\(\\lambda<0\\) (\\(\\varepsilon<0\\)), \\(\\delta\\phi<0\\): the periapsis **retrocedes** (retrograde precession).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.   Closedness of a non\u2011circular bound orbit  \n\nA bound orbit will repeat exactly after a finite number of radial periods iff the azimuthal advance per radial period is a rational multiple of \\(2\\pi\\); equivalently the factor \\(\\nu\\) must be a rational number.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Closed orbit }\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n       \\nu = \\frac{p}{q}\\;(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\nu^{2}=1+\\dfrac{m\\lambda}{\\ell^{2}}\\),\n\n\\[\n1+\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\ell^{2}} = \\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right)^{2}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\ell^{2}} = \\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right)^{2}-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus a non\u2011circular bound orbit is closed **iff** the ratio \\((m\\lambda)/\\ell^{2}\\) can be written as the difference of two rational squares.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Summary of results**\n\n| Item | Result |\n|------|--------|\n| Effective potential | \\(V_{\\rm eff}(r)=-\\dfrac{k}{r}+\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2r^{2}}+\\dfrac{m\\ell^{2}}{2r^{4}}\\) |\n| Circular\u2011orbit radius | \\(r_{0}= \\dfrac{\\lambda}{k}+ \\dfrac{m\\ell^{2}}{k^{2}}\\) |\n| Stability condition | \\(r_{0}<\\tfrac32\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\\lambda+\\dfrac{m\\ell^{2}}{k}<\\dfrac{3k}{2}\\) |\n| Binet equation | \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{{\\rm d}^{2}u}{{\\rm d}\\phi^{2}}+\\nu^{2}u=\\frac{mk}{\\ell^{2}},\\qquad \\nu^{2}=1+\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\ell^{2}}\\) |\n| Bounded\u2011orbit form | \\(u(\\phi)=u_{0}[1+e\\cos(\\nu(\\phi-\\phi_{0}))]\\) with \\(u_{0}=mk/(\\ell^{2}\\nu^{2})\\) |\n| Azimuthal step between periapsides | \\(\\Delta\\phi=2\\pi/\\nu\\) |\n| Periapsis precession per radial period | \\(\\delta\\phi=2\\pi(1-1/\\nu)\\approx\\pi\\,m\\lambda/\\ell^{2}\\) |\n| Direction of precession | Prograde for \\(\\lambda>0\\); retrograde for \\(\\lambda<0\\) |\n| Closed\u2011orbit condition | \\(\\nu=p/q\\) (rational) \u21d4 \\(1+m\\lambda/\\ell^{2}=(p/q)^{2}\\) |\n\nThese formulas give the complete description of the motion in the modified Kepler potential.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer multiple subquestions about central potential V = -k/r + \u03bb/(2 r^2). Provide effective potential, condition for stable circular orbit, radius.\n\nEffective potential includes kinetic term (\u2113^2)/(2 m r^2). So V_eff = -k/r + \u03bb/(2 r^2) + \u2113^2/(2 m r^2).\n\nSet derivative zero: dV_eff/dr = 0 => k/r^2 - (\u03bb + \u2113^2/m)/r^3 =0? Let's compute:\n\nV_eff = -k r^{-1} + (\u03bb/2 + \u2113^2/(2m)) r^{-2}. Derivative w.r.t r: dV/dr = k r^{-2} - 2 (\u03bb/2 + \u2113^2/(2m)) r^{-3} = k / r^2 - (\u03bb + \u2113^2/m)/ r^3.\n\nSet zero: k / r^2 = (\u03bb + \u2113^2/m)/ r^3 \u2192 multiply both sides by r^3: k r = \u03bb + \u2113^2/m \u2192 r = (\u03bb + \u2113^2/m)/k . That's candidate radius r0.\n\nStability requires second derivative positive at that point: d\u00b2V/dr\u00b2 >0.\n\nCompute second derivative: d\u00b2V/dr\u00b2 = -2k r^{-3} + 3 (\u03bb + \u2113^2/m) r^{-4}. Evaluate at r=r0.\n\nPlug r0 expression maybe easier: Use condition k r0 = \u03bb + \u2113^2/m.\n\nThen d\u00b2V/dr\u00b2|_{r0}= -2k / r0^3 + 3 (\u03bb + \u2113^2/m)/ r0^4 = -2k / r0^3 + 3 k / r0^4 (since \u03bb+\u2113^2/m = k r0). Multiply numerator: = (-2k r0 + 3k)/ r0^4 = k (3 - 2 r0)/ r0^4.\n\nThus stability requires 3 - 2 r0 >0 \u2192 r0 < 3/2.\n\nBut r0 expressed as (\u03bb + \u2113^2/m)/k. So condition: (\u03bb + \u2113^2/m)/k < 3/2 \u2192 \u03bb + \u2113^2/m < 3k/2.\n\nAlso note r0 must be positive; also denominator not zero.\n\nNow part (2): Binet equation. Using u = 1/r, standard result: d\u00b2u/d\u03c6\u00b2 + u = - (m/\u2113^2) * (1/u^2) F(1/u) where F(r) = -dV/dr. Actually Binet formula: (d\u00b2u/d\u03c6\u00b2)+u = - (m/(\u2113^2 u^2)) [F(1/u)], where F = -dV/dr.\n\nGiven V(r) = -k/r + \u03bb/(2 r^2). Compute force: F = -dV/dr = -d/dr(-k r^{-1}+ \u03bb/2 r^{-2}) = -(k r^{-2} - \u03bb r^{-3}) = -k/r^2 + \u03bb/r^3.\n\nAlternatively we can use effective potential but Binet uses actual potential.\n\nSo plug into Binet: d\u00b2u/d\u03c6\u00b2 + u = - (m/(\u2113^2 u^2)) [-k u^2 + \u03bb u^3] because replace r = 1/u.\n\nCompute: -k u^2 + \u03bb u^3 = u^2(-k + \u03bb u). Then divide by u^2 gives -(m/\u2113^2)(-k + \u03bb u) = (m/\u2113^2)(k - \u03bb u).\n\nThus equation: d\u00b2u/d\u03c6\u00b2 + u = (m k)/(\u2113^2) - (m \u03bb)/(\u2113^2) u.\n\nBring terms: d\u00b2u/d\u03c6\u00b2 + u + (m \u03bb)/(\u2113^2) u = (m k)/\u2113^2.\n\n=> d\u00b2u/d\u03c6\u00b2 + [1 + (m \u03bb)/\u2113^2] u = (m k)/\u2113^2.\n\nDefine \u03bd^2 = 1 + (m \u03bb)/\u2113^2. Then homogeneous solution plus particular constant.\n\nThus differential eq: d\u00b2u/d\u03c6\u00b2 + \u03bd^2 u = (m k)/\u2113^2.\n\nThat's part (2).\n\nPart (3): Solve for bound orbit. The general solution: u = A cos(\u03bd \u03c6) + B sin(\u03bd \u03c6) + C, where C = (m k)/(\u2113^2 \u03bd^2). Choose phase shift to write as amplitude times cosine.\n\nLet\u2019s set u = u0 (1 + e cos[\u03bd(\u03c6-\u03c60)]) . Need to match.\n\nStandard approach: Write u = u0 + u0 e cos[\u03bd(\u03c6-\u03c60)], where u0 = (m k)/(\u2113^2 \u03bd^2). Indeed from particular solution constant.\n\nCheck: If we take u = u0 (1 + e cos[\u03bd(\u03c6-\u03c60)]), then expand: u = u0 + u0 e cos[\u03bd(\u03c6-\u03c60)]. This matches solution with amplitude e u0.\n\nThus u0 = (m k)/(\u2113^2 \u03bd^2). And \u03bd defined above.\n\nNow find increase in azimuthal angle between successive periapses. Periapsis occurs when cos term = 1 (i.e., maximum u). Successive periapses correspond to increase \u0394\u03c6 such that \u03bd \u0394\u03c6 = 2\u03c0. Therefore \u0394\u03c6 = 2\u03c0/\u03bd.\n\nThus azimuthal angle between periapses = 2\u03c0/\u03bd.\n\nPart (4): Precession per radial period. Radial period corresponds to one full oscillation in r, i.e., one revolution in \u03c6 of amount 2\u03c0. But due to \u03bd \u2260 1, the true anomaly advances more/less than 2\u03c0 per radial period. The precession per radial period \u03b4 = 2\u03c0 - 2\u03c0/\u03bd = 2\u03c0 (1 - 1/\u03bd). Equivalent to advance per orbit relative to Kepler.\n\nNow compute \u03bd = sqrt(1 + (m \u03bb)/\u2113^2). So 1/\u03bd = 1/ sqrt(1 + \u03b5) where \u03b5 = (m \u03bb)/\u2113^2.\n\nPrecession per radial period: \u0394\u03c6_prec = 2\u03c0 (1 - 1/\u03bd). For small \u03b5, expand: \u03bd \u2248 1 + \u03b5/2 - \u03b5^2/8 ... then 1/\u03bd \u2248 1 - \u03b5/2 + \u03b5^2/4 ... So 1 - 1/\u03bd \u2248 \u03b5/2 - \u03b5^2/4 + ... Leading order: \u03b5/2.\n\nThus \u0394\u03c6_prec \u2248 2\u03c0 * (\u03b5/2) = \u03c0 \u03b5 = \u03c0 (m \u03bb)/\u2113^2.\n\nSign determines direction: if \u03bb>0, \u03b5>0, precession positive meaning prograde (advances forward). If \u03bb<0, \u03b5 negative, precession negative meaning retrograde (retrograde). However need to check sign conventions: In our definition, \u03bd = sqrt(1 + \u03b5). If \u03b5 negative but magnitude less than 1 to keep \u03bd real, then \u03bd <1, so 1/\u03bd >1, thus 1 - 1/\u03bd negative -> precession negative (retrograde). Good.\n\nNow condition for closed orbit: orbit repeats after integer number of revolutions: need \u03bd rational number p/q? Actually for closed orbit, after some integer number of radial periods, the angular advance should be an integer multiple of 2\u03c0. That means \u03bd must be rational: \u03bd = p/q with p,q integers (in lowest terms). Because after q radial periods, total angular change = q*2\u03c0/\u03bd = 2\u03c0 p, an integer multiple of 2\u03c0. Conversely, if \u03bd irrational, never close exactly.\n\nThus necessary and sufficient condition: \u03bd is a rational number. Since \u03bd = sqrt(1 + (m \u03bb)/\u2113^2). So require 1 + (m \u03bb)/\u2113^2 = (p/q)^2 rational square. Equivalent to (m \u03bb)/\u2113^2 = r^2 -1 where r = p/q rational. So condition: (m \u03bb)/\u2113^2 = (p/q)^2 -1 with p,q integers.\n\nProvide statement.\n\nNow compile final answer with clear steps.\n\n"
